IDEA 1: Contrastive HiResCAM selection — Let \(X_i\) be student patch \(i\)’s penultimate-block output and \(m=z_a-z_b\), where \(a,b\) are its two highest-logit classes; compute \(g_i=\partial m/\partial X_i\) using one `autograd.grad(..., retain_graph=True)` call.
Keep the K largest \(s_i=|\sum_c X_{ic}g_{ic}|\): a local attribution of evidence affecting the species distinction in either direction, without labels or spatial rules. — basis: Draelos & Carin, [HiResCAM](https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.08891), 2020/2021: elementwise activation–gradient attribution avoids spatial gradient averaging; demonstrated on CNNs, whose faithfulness guarantee does **not** establish this ViT adaptation. Preserving discriminative bird details is the hypothesis; CUB gains remain untested. — closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [Prompt Condensation](https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.17223), Neural Networks 2024, uses Taylor importance for learned prompts; this ranks image patches per example by a label-free class margin to select teacher inputs. — extra FLOPs: approximately 0.25–0.85 GFLOPs/image for one final-block input-gradient pass at student width 192–384, plus negligible scoring; no parameter gradients accumulated.

IDEA 2: CLS reconstruction pursuit — Recover last-block values \(v_{hi}\) from student penultimate tokens and value-projection weights; read CLS attention \(a_{hi}\). Define the full message \(u_h=\sum_{i=0}^{196}a_{hi}v_{hi}\) and subset message \(\hat u_h(S)=\frac{\sum_{i\in S\cup\{0\}}a_{hi}v_{hi}}{\sum_{i\in S\cup\{0\}}a_{hi}}\), with \(0=\mathrm{CLS}\).
Starting with \(S=\varnothing\), score each remaining patch by \(E(S)-E(S\cup\{i\})\), where \(E(S)=\sum_h\|u_h-\hat u_h(S)\|^2\), and greedily add K patches: a deletion-based explanation of the student’s CLS aggregation with Q/K/V held fixed. — inspired by: Mallat & Zhang, [Matching Pursuits With Time-Frequency Dictionaries](https://www.di.ens.fr/~mallat/papiers/MallatPursuit93.pdf), IEEE Transactions on Signal Processing, 1993. — why it transfers: Its demonstrated sparse signal decomposition supplies the strongest transferable idea: select what explains the remaining reconstruction error; here complementary value contributions may preserve evidence that attention top-K duplicates, although student-to-teacher transfer and CUB superiority remain untested. — queries tried: interpretable image classification contrastive explanations feature attribution competing classes; diverse evidence selection conditional information experimental design feature representations; sparse approximation matching pursuit reconstruct signal dictionary atoms explanatory subset; prototype selection explain predictions kernel mean matching ProtoDash; experimental design determinant feature diversity sensor selection information gain; nonnegative matrix factorization neural activations discover semantic parts visual explanations — closest prior token-pruning work and difference: [ResPrune](https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.21105) selects text-conditioned embedding subspaces; this explains an actual attention computation through subset-renormalized message fidelity, without fitted reconstruction coefficients, merging, or spatial rules. — extra FLOPs: \(2Nd^2+O(KNd)\), including recomputing values; roughly 0.02–0.08 GFLOPs/image for \(N=197,\ K=29,\ d=192–384\).